/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - LiDAR stands for light detection and ranging.
   - R is slant range in metres; c is the speed of light in vacuum, 299,792,458 m/s; t is two-way travel time in seconds.
   - The main parts are a laser, a scanner mirror, a detector, a timer, a GNSS receiver and an IMU.
   - Airborne LiDAR flies on an aircraft or helicopter.
   - A pulse can hit leaves before ground.
   - Georeferencing turns ranges and angles into map coordinates.
   - Range errors come from timer resolution and pulse shape.
   - Classification and point cloud analysis sit in a GIS or CAD workflow.
   - A scan is not a map until points are classified and checked against control.
*/
