/* book Worked example 2: Q: An airborne LiDAR flies at 1000 m above ground at 60 m/s, scans ±20° across track, and fires 200 000 pulses per second. Find the swath width and the average point density on flat ground.
   1. Swath width W = 2 H tan(θ/2) with half-angle 20°: W = 2 × 1000 × tan 20° = 2000 × 0.36397 = 727.9 m.
   2. Ground area swept each second = speed × swath = 60 × 727.9 = 43 676 m².
   3. Point density = pulses per second ÷ area per second = 200 000/43 676 = 4.58 points per m².
   4. Average spacing between points ≈ 1/√4.58 = 0.47 m.
   5. Flying lower, more slowly or with a narrower scan raises the density; a 50 % side overlap between strips roughly doubles it again.
   Answer: Swath 728 m, average density about 4.6 points per m², spacing about 0.47 m. */
